# Deploy to Cloudflare Workers

Until the user says to stop, every completed site or UI change is published to both Workers. A push to `master` publishes both:

- Live Worker `hiweb-marketing-ui` (https://www.hiwebmarketing.com): the GitHub Actions workflow "Deploy Worker" on every push to `master`, plus Cloudflare Workers Builds through the Studio rebuild hook.
- Staging Worker `hiweb-marketing-ui-staging` (https://staging.hiweb.com.mx): the GitHub Actions workflow "Deploy staging".

## Normal flow

1. Run `git pull --autostash` first. Teammates push to `master` often.
2. Commit only the files you changed for this task. Never stage `dist/`, `fonts/`, or other untracked files you did not create. Use the repo's Conventional Commits style (`feat(...)`, `fix(...)`, `perf(...)`).
3. `git push`.
4. Wait for both builds to finish. They take about 5 minutes. Check them:

```
$sha = git rev-parse --short HEAD
(gh api "repos/hiwebapps/hiweb-marketing-ui/commits/$sha/check-runs" | ConvertFrom-Json).check_runs | ForEach-Object { "$($_.name) = $(if ($_.conclusion) { $_.conclusion } else { $_.status })" }
```

`Workers Builds: hiweb-marketing-ui` is live. `deploy` is staging.

5. Confirm https://www.hiwebmarketing.com and https://staging.hiweb.com.mx. Staging returns 401 until the staging password is sent.

## When an automatic build fails

The agent cannot read Workers Builds logs: the Cloudflare connection lacks `Workers CI Read`. Ask the user to copy the log from the dashboard (Workers & Pages → `hiweb-marketing-ui` → Builds).

- "error occurred while fetching repository" is an intermittent Cloudflare-to-GitHub failure. Ask the user to click "Retry build", or run the manual live deploy below.
- For a staging failure, read it with `gh run view <id> --log-failed`.

Manual deploy, only for a Worker whose automatic build failed:

1. Live. Unset staging env vars first, or this build is published to the wrong Worker:

```
Remove-Item Env:PUBLIC_SITE_ENV, Env:CLOUDFLARE_ENV, Env:PUBLIC_SITE_URL -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
$env:PUBLIC_SITE_URL='https://www.hiwebmarketing.com'
npx astro build; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { exit $LASTEXITCODE }
npx wrangler deploy --keep-vars
```

2. Staging. `CLOUDFLARE_ENV=staging` has to be set during the Astro build. The adapter writes `dist/server/wrangler.json` for that environment. Without it, `wrangler deploy --env staging` publishes the bundle to the live Worker.

```
Remove-Item Env:PUBLIC_SITE_ENV, Env:CLOUDFLARE_ENV, Env:PUBLIC_SITE_URL -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
$env:PUBLIC_SITE_ENV='staging'; $env:PUBLIC_SITE_URL='https://staging.hiweb.com.mx'; $env:CLOUDFLARE_ENV='staging'
npx astro build; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { exit $LASTEXITCODE }
npx wrangler deploy --env staging --keep-vars
Remove-Item Env:PUBLIC_SITE_ENV, Env:CLOUDFLARE_ENV, Env:PUBLIC_SITE_URL -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
```

## Always

- A manual deploy builds the local working copy, not `master`. Push first so the next automatic build does not overwrite it with older code.
- Always pass `--keep-vars` so existing Worker secrets stay in place.
- Do not run the live build, the staging build, and `npx sanity deploy` at the same time. All three write `dist/`.
- Do not deploy Studio unless the change is a Sanity schema or Studio UI the editors need.
- Keep `PUBLIC_SITE_INDEXABLE` false.
- If the browser still shows the previous version, tell the user to reload with Ctrl+F5.
